<h1>Endpoints Config Types</h1>
<p>A test says which machines it needs through
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#declaring">endpoint fields</a> on its config class. The
	field is configuration like any other: the same test body runs on the local machine or across a lab depending
	on the values chosen in the run panel. The <strong>Endpoints</strong> group of the tutorial suite declares one
	test per way of choosing machines, and this page follows them with the endpoints registered on
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/endpoints-management/">the previous page</a>.</p>
<p>Every test in this group needs its <strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>, and the flows and steps are the
	ones from <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/structure-and-lifecycle/">Structure and Lifecycle</a>. Nothing
	in them mentions a machine; where they run is decided by the fields described here.</p>

<h2 id="a-single-endpoint">A single endpoint</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_default_endpoint_and_package_upload/") }}
<p>Every test config inherits a <code>test_endpoint</code> field that defaults to <code>localhost</code>. It is
	hidden in the run panel until a test declares it, which <strong>Default Endpoint and Package Upload</strong>
	does with one line:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Test Endpoint")') }}
<p>Select the test and click the edit button at the right of <strong>Test Endpoint</strong>. The dialog lists
	the registered endpoints; choose the Linux machine:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/single-endpoint-config.png", "Default Endpoint and Package Upload run panel with Test Endpoint set to GMG-TEST-3, Upload Test Package set to If Missing, Update Endpoint App set to On Mismatch, and the Maze Executable Folder showing Endpoints All and Upload If Missing", "large") }}
<p>Two rows sit under the endpoint field. <strong>Upload Test Package</strong> decides whether the suite's own
	code is sent to the endpoint before the run, and <strong>Update Endpoint App</strong> whether the endpoint
	application is brought to the controller's version first. Both default to doing so only when needed. The
	<strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong> row has grown an <strong>Endpoints</strong> line and an
	<strong>Upload</strong> choice of its own, because the field is a package folder that travels to the machine
	where it is used; the next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/packages-uploading/">Packages
	Uploading</a>, is about that.</p>
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, then <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and
	confirm. Open the run's Details when it finishes:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/single-endpoint-details.png", "Default Endpoint and Package Upload Details view with every step row carrying GMG-TEST-3 in its Endpoints column and a passing status", "large") }}
<p>Every step row now carries <strong>GMG-TEST-3</strong> in its <strong>Endpoints</strong> column. The steps
	ran on the Linux machine, with the Windows build of the Maze application replaced by the Linux one the package
	folder also holds, and their results came back to the controller exactly as they did from
	<strong>localhost</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="endpoints-with-roles">Endpoints with roles</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_multiple_endpoints_and_files_transfer/") }}
<p>A test that needs two machines with different jobs declares two fields.
	<strong>Multiple Endpoints and Files Transfer</strong> generates mazes on the first and solves them on the
	second, moving the files between them in the meantime:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('endpoint_1 = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Endpoint 1")
endpoint_2 = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Endpoint 2")') }}
<p>The flows pass an <code>endpoint</code> argument to <code>run_step</code> so that each step goes to the machine
	for its role, and the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#transfer">file transfer</a> between
	the two is a framework call the flow makes between the steps. Set <strong>Endpoint 1</strong> to the Windows
	machine and <strong>Endpoint 2</strong> to the macOS machine, update the plan and run. The Details show the
	split:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/two-endpoints-details.png", "Multiple Endpoints and Files Transfer Details view with the Delete and Generate steps of every flow marked GMG-TEST-1 and the Load / Solve steps marked GMG-TEST-5", "large") }}
<p>Within each flow the <strong>Delete Existing Maze File</strong> and <strong>Generate / Save</strong> steps ran
	on <strong>GMG-TEST-1</strong> and the <strong>Load / Solve</strong> steps on <strong>GMG-TEST-5</strong>. The
	configuration table on the left records the plan and run values of both fields with their ports, so a run can
	always be traced back to the machines it used.</p>

<h2 id="a-set-of-endpoints">A set of endpoints</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_multi_select_endpoints/") }}
<p><strong>Multi-Select Endpoints</strong> keeps one generator and takes any number of solvers:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.Endpoint(default="localhost", label="Test Endpoint")
aux_endpoints = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectEndpoints(default=("localhost",), label="Aux Endpoints")') }}
<p>A <code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code> field holds a tuple of endpoints, and the test body loops over it, running
	the three transfer flows against each solver in turn. Choose the Windows machine as the
	<strong>Test Endpoint</strong> and the Linux and macOS machines as <strong>Aux Endpoints</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/multi-select-config.png", "Multi-Select Endpoints run panel with Test Endpoint set to GMG-TEST-1 and the Aux Endpoints multi-select field listing GMG-TEST-3 and GMG-TEST-5", "large") }}
<p>The field lists its selected endpoints one per line. It is still one run: the four cases each visit both
	solvers, and the endpoints play parts in one scenario rather than repeating it.</p>

<h2 id="fan-out">Fan-out</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_endpoint_fanout/") }}
<p><strong>Endpoint Fanout</strong> runs the flows of the first test, but redeclares the inherited field as a
	fan-out:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('test_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.EndpointFanout(default=("localhost",), label="Test Endpoint")') }}
<p>An <code>EndpointFanout</code> field also holds a tuple, but each selected endpoint gets a run of its own.
	Select all three machines:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-config.png", "Endpoint Fanout run panel with the Test Endpoint (Fanout) field listing GMG-TEST-1, GMG-TEST-3 and GMG-TEST-5 and the counter reporting Total Cases 12", "large") }}
<p><strong>Total Cases</strong> reads 12 for a matrix of four cases: the four cases three times over, once per
	machine. Update the plan and run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-runs-tree.png", "Execution tree with the Endpoint Fanout test planning 12 cases and holding three Test Run rows of four passed cases each, one per selected endpoint", "large") }}
<p>The test row plans 12 and holds three <strong>Test Run</strong> rows, one per endpoint, that ran at the same
	time. Fan-out is the way to run one verification everywhere; the multi-select field is the way to let several
	machines take part in one run.</p>

<h3 id="queued-runs">Queued runs</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_endpoint_fanout_queued/") }}
<p><strong>Endpoint Fanout - Queued</strong> mixes the two kinds. <strong>Endpoint 1</strong>, the generator,
	is a plain endpoint, and <strong>Endpoint 2</strong>, the solver, is a fan-out. Choose the Windows machine as
	the generator and the Linux and macOS machines as solvers, then run:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-queued-tree.png", "Execution tree during the Endpoint Fanout - Queued run, with one Test Run row running and the second row waiting behind it", "large") }}
<p>Two runs are created, but they do not start together. Both need the generator, an endpoint is held by the run
	that uses it, and so the second run waits until the first releases it. The panel's <strong>Shared</strong>
	option on an endpoint field lifts that rule for machines that can serve several runs at once; the framework
	reference describes it under
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#endpoint-options">field options</a>.</p>

<h3 id="permutations">Permutations</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_endpoints/test_endpoint_fanout_permutations/") }}
<p>When both roles are fan-outs, the runs are the combinations of the two selections. Select the Windows and the
	Linux machine for both <strong>Endpoint 1</strong> and <strong>Endpoint 2</strong> of
	<strong>Endpoint Fanout - Permutations</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/endpoints-config-types/fanout-permutations-config.png", "Endpoint Fanout - Permutations run panel with GMG-TEST-1 and GMG-TEST-3 selected in both fan-out fields and a Fanout Endpoints Permutations table listing the four combinations, all ticked, with Total Cases 16", "large") }}
<p>A <strong>Fanout Endpoints Permutations</strong> table appears with one row per combination, four here, each
	with a check mark in <strong>Select</strong>. Clearing a row drops that combination from the plan, which is how
	a machine can be excluded from solving its own mazes without touching either field. With all four selected, the
	four cases run 16 times, and each combination is a run of its own.</p>

<p>The next page, <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/packages-uploading/">Packages Uploading</a>, follows the
	package fields that carried the Maze application to these machines, and shows how a field is tied to the
	endpoints that need its files.</p>
